"""Scene definitions for the TLS Servers in Practice with nginx and Caddy teaching video (generated from concept.toml)."""

SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='TLS Servers in Practice with nginx and Caddy',
         body=None,
         narration='Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains setting up T L S, transport layer security, on two real web servers, nginx and Caddy. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] Every T L S server is a handful of choices: which certificate, which protocol versions, whether to redirect plain http, and whether to send H S T S. [[slnc 300]] In nginx, each choice is a line you write. [[slnc 300]] Caddy makes most of them for you, including its own certificates. [[slnc 600]] Think of two shops: one where you fit every lock yourself, and one that arrives with locks fitted, and you must still know which. [[slnc 600]] Our example is an online store.'),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=['An online store wants its pages', 'served only over HTTPS,', 'with certificates from its own authority.', '', 'The demo starts real nginx and real Caddy', 'in front of the same shop,', 'and changes one line at a time.'],
         narration='Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] An online store wants its pages served only over https, with certificates from its own authority. [[slnc 500]] The demo starts a real nginx and a real Caddy in front of the same small shop. [[slnc 500]] A small https client, written with the J D K, reads what a visitor would see, while the demo changes one line at a time.'),
    dict(key='03-words', kind='bullets', title='The Words',
         body=['Certificate: a name and a key, signed by an authority', 'Root: an authority the client already trusts', 'Handshake: client and server agree, before any page', 'Redirect: an answer that says go to this address', 'Response header: a line of extra facts with a page'],
         narration='Before the demos, the words. [[slnc 500]] A certificate is a name and a public key, signed by an authority. [[slnc 500]] A root is an authority that the client already trusts, kept in its own list. [[slnc 500]] The handshake is where client and server agree on a version and check the certificate, before any page is sent. [[slnc 500]] A redirect is an answer that says: ask at this other address. [[slnc 500]] And a response header is a line of extra facts sent with a page, like strict transport security.'),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['TLS: Transport Layer Security: the encrypted, checked connection under https', 'HTTP, HTTPS: Hypertext Transfer Protocol; HTTPS is HTTP over TLS', 'HSTS: HTTP Strict Transport Security: a header that says use https only', 'OCSP: Online Certificate Status Protocol: asks if a certificate is revoked', "SSL: Secure Sockets Layer: TLS's old name, kept in nginx's ssl_ lines", "JDK: Java Development Kit: Java's own TLS client is used here"],
         narration='A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] T L S is transport layer security: the encrypted and checked connection that https runs on. [[slnc 400]] H T T P is the hypertext transfer protocol, the language of web pages. H T T P S is the same, inside T L S. [[slnc 400]] H S T S is H T T P strict transport security: a header that tells the browser to use https only, for a time. [[slnc 400]] O C S P is the online certificate status protocol. A responder answers whether a certificate has been revoked. [[slnc 400]] S S L is secure sockets layer, the old name of T L S. nginx still uses it in the names of its lines. [[slnc 400]] J D K is the Java development kit. Its own T L S client is used in this demo.'),
    dict(key='05-letters-2', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=['SNI: Server Name Indication: the name a client asks for in the handshake', "ECC: Elliptic Curve Cryptography: the kind of key Caddy's authority uses", 'URL: Uniform Resource Locator: a web address'],
         narration="S N I is server name indication: the name the client sends in the handshake. [[slnc 400]] E C C is elliptic curve cryptography, the kind of key that Caddy's local authority uses. [[slnc 400]] U R L is uniform resource locator: a web address."),
    dict(key='06-two-servers', kind='figure', title='The same choices on two servers',
         body='two_servers',
         narration="Let's see the whole idea on one picture. [[slnc 400]] Each row is one choice a T L S server makes. [[slnc 300]] In the middle, nginx: each choice is a line you write, from the certificate files to the add header line for H S T S. [[slnc 700]] On the right, Caddy, where nobody wrote a line. [[slnc 300]] It made its own certificate, for twelve hours. [[slnc 300]] It serves T L S one point two. [[slnc 300]] It redirects with three oh eight, and says only Caddy. [[slnc 300]] And it sends no H S T S. [[slnc 700]] Every value here comes from the demo's real output. [[slnc 300]] The same five choices, made by hand or by default."),
    dict(key='07-hsts-year', kind='figure', title='What the HSTS header promises',
         body='hsts_year',
         narration='One more picture before the demos, for the header that is hardest to take back. [[slnc 400]] Over https, the response carries strict transport security, with a max age. [[slnc 300]] Over plain http, nothing: there it would be ignored anyway. [[slnc 700]] The max age is in seconds. [[slnc 300]] Thirty one million, five hundred and thirty six thousand seconds, divided by eighty six thousand four hundred seconds in a day, is three hundred and sixty five days. [[slnc 700]] For a year, a browser that saw it will use only https for this name, and for every name under it. [[slnc 300]] That is why you test it before you send it.'),
    dict(key='08-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='Three clients, two checks, one certificate',
         body='chain_check',
         narration="First demo: nginx with a certificate from the local authority. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the certificate nginx sends. [[slnc 300]] Its name is w w w dot shop dot localhost. [[slnc 300]] It was signed by Shop Lab Root, the lab's own root, and it lasts twenty four hours. [[slnc 700]] A client makes two checks. [[slnc 300]] First: is the signer one of my roots, and does its signature verify? [[slnc 300]] Second: is the name I asked for in the certificate? [[slnc 700]] The first client trusts the lab's root, and asks for w w w. [[slnc 300]] Both checks pass, and the page comes back over T L S one point three. [[slnc 600]] The second client has only the default roots of the Java development kit. [[slnc 300]] Shop Lab Root is not among them, so it hangs up. [[slnc 600]] The third client trusts the right root, but asks for pay dot shop dot localhost. [[slnc 300]] That name is not in the certificate, so it hangs up too."),
    dict(key='09-act-1-1', kind='console', title='Act One — nginx with a certificate from the local authority',
         body="ONE. nginx with a certificate from the local authority.\n  THE CERTIFICATE (made with step, signed by the lab's root):\n    subject   www.shop.localhost\n    names     [www.shop.localhost]\n    issuer    Shop Lab Root\n    lifetime  24 hours\n  THE NGINX LINES (the https server block):\n    listen 127.0.0.1:<https-port> ssl;\n    ssl_certificate <work>/ca/www.crt;\n    ssl_certificate_key <work>/ca/www.key;",
         narration='Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The certificate: one name, issued by Shop Lab Root, twenty four hours. [[slnc 400]] And the nginx lines that serve it: a listen line with s s l, the certificate file, and its key.'),
    dict(key='10-act-1-2', kind='console', title='Act One — nginx with a certificate from the local authority',
         body="ONE. (continued)\n  THE REQUESTS (the roots the client trusts, the name it asks):\n    1  the lab's root    www.shop.localhost\n    2  default roots     www.shop.localhost\n    3  the lab's root    pay.shop.localhost\n  THE CHECKS, BY HAND (signed by a trusted root? name listed?):\n    1  Shop Lab Root trusted: yes; www.shop.localhost listed: yes\n    2  Shop Lab Root trusted: no\n    3  Shop Lab Root trusted: yes; pay.shop.localhost listed: no\n  THE RESPONSES:\n    1  200 over TLSv1.3\n    2  refused: no trusted root signed it\n    3  refused: the certificate is not for that name",
         narration='The three requests, the checks done by hand, and the responses. [[slnc 400]] Only the first passes both checks, and gets two hundred over T L S one point three. [[slnc 400]] The by-hand checks agree with the real handshakes.'),
    dict(key='11-act-1-3', kind='console', title='Act One — nginx with a certificate from the local authority',
         body='ONE. (continued)\n  THE REQUEST (plain http):\n    GET http://www.shop.localhost:<http-port>/cart\n  THE RESPONSE:\n    301  Location: https://www.shop.localhost:<https-port>/cart\n  BEFORE THE FIX (server_tokens on, the default):\n    Server: nginx/<version>\n  AFTER THE FIX (server_tokens off;):\n    Server: nginx',
         narration='Plain http is answered with three oh one, and a location on https. [[slnc 500]] Before the fix, the server header gives nginx and its exact version. [[slnc 300]] After server tokens off, it says only nginx.'),
    dict(key='12-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='The highest version both sides speak',
         body='protocol_overlap',
         narration='Second demo: which protocol versions it speaks. [[slnc 400]] In a handshake, the client says which versions it can speak. [[slnc 300]] The server picks the highest one that both of them know. [[slnc 700]] On the left, the default line: T L S one point two and one point three. [[slnc 300]] The old client offers only one point two. [[slnc 300]] One point two is in common, so it is served. [[slnc 300]] The new client offers one point three, and gets one point three. [[slnc 700]] On the right, one changed line: one point three only. [[slnc 300]] For the old client, nothing is in common. [[slnc 300]] The handshake fails before a single page is sent. [[slnc 300]] That is the price of the stricter line: every client that cannot speak one point three.'),
    dict(key='13-act-2-1', kind='console', title='Act Two — Which protocol versions it speaks',
         body='TWO. Which protocol versions it speaks.\n  THE CLIENTS (what each one offers):\n    old client  [TLSv1.2]\n    new client  [TLSv1.3]\n  BEFORE THE CHANGE (the default line):\n    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;\n  THE AGREEMENT, BY HAND (the highest version both speak):\n    old client  [TLSv1.2, TLSv1.3] and [TLSv1.2] -> TLSv1.2\n    new client  [TLSv1.2, TLSv1.3] and [TLSv1.3] -> TLSv1.3\n  THE RESPONSES:\n    old client  200 over TLSv1.2\n    new client  200 over TLSv1.3',
         narration='Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The two clients, and the default line. [[slnc 400]] The agreement worked out by hand: one point two for the old client, one point three for the new one. [[slnc 400]] And the real responses: two hundred for both.'),
    dict(key='14-act-2-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — Which protocol versions it speaks',
         body='TWO. (continued)\n  AFTER THE CHANGE (one line):\n    ssl_protocols TLSv1.3;\n  THE AGREEMENT, BY HAND (the highest version both speak):\n    old client  [TLSv1.3] and [TLSv1.2] -> none\n    new client  [TLSv1.3] and [TLSv1.3] -> TLSv1.3\n  THE RESPONSES:\n    old client  refused: no protocol version in common\n    new client  200 over TLSv1.3',
         narration='After the change, to one point three only. [[slnc 400]] By hand, the old client has nothing in common. [[slnc 400]] And the real server refuses it: no protocol version in common.'),
    dict(key='15-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title="A location's own add_header replaces the server's",
         body='header_trap',
         narration="Third demo: H S T S, and the add header trap. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the server block, with one add header line for strict transport security. [[slnc 300]] Inside it, the account location, with an add header line of its own, for X frame options. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the rule. [[slnc 300]] nginx gives a location the server's add header lines only when the location has none. [[slnc 300]] The account location has one, so only its own line applies. [[slnc 700]] The real response proves it. [[slnc 300]] X frame options is there. [[slnc 300]] Strict transport security is gone, without any warning. [[slnc 600]] After the fix, the line is repeated in the location, and both headers come back."),
    dict(key='16-act-3-1', kind='console', title='Act Three — HSTS, and the add_header trap',
         body='THREE. HSTS, and the add_header trap.\n  BEFORE THE FIX (no add_header line):\n    GET / over https  Strict-Transport-Security: (none)\n  THE LINE ADDED TO THE SERVER BLOCK:\n    add_header Strict-Transport-Security\n      "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains" always;\n  AFTER THE FIX (Strict-Transport-Security in the response):\n    GET / over https  max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains\n    GET / over http   (none)\n  THE max-age, WORKED OUT:\n    31536000 s / 86400 s a day = 365 days\n    includeSubDomains: yes, so every name under www.shop.localhost too',
         narration='Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] Before the fix, no strict transport security. [[slnc 400]] The line added to the server block. [[slnc 400]] After it, the header comes back over https, and not over plain http. [[slnc 400]] Its max age, worked out: thirty one million, five hundred and thirty six thousand seconds, divided by the seconds in a day, is three hundred and sixty five days.'),
    dict(key='17-act-3-2', kind='console', title='Act Three — HSTS, and the add_header trap',
         body="THREE. (continued)\n  THE TRAP (/account has an add_header of its own):\n    location /account {\n        add_header X-Frame-Options DENY always;\n    }\n  THE RULE, BY HAND (a location's own lines replace the server's):\n    server    [Strict-Transport-Security]\n    location  [X-Frame-Options]\n    applied   [X-Frame-Options]",
         narration="The trap: the account location, with its own add header line. [[slnc 400]] The rule worked out by hand: the location's own list wins."),
    dict(key='18-act-3-3', kind='console', title='Act Three — HSTS, and the add_header trap',
         body="THREE. (continued)\n  THE RESPONSE FROM GET /account:\n    X-Frame-Options: DENY\n    Strict-Transport-Security: (none)  <- THE SERVER'S LINE IS LOST\n  AFTER THE FIX (the line repeated in the location):\n    X-Frame-Options: DENY\n    Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains",
         narration='The real response: X frame options, and no strict transport security. [[slnc 400]] With the line repeated in the location, both are there.'),
    dict(key='19-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title='A short Caddyfile, a whole chain behind it',
         body='caddy_chain',
         narration="Fourth demo: Caddy makes its own certificates. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the site block of the Caddyfile. [[slnc 300]] A name, an address, and a reply. [[slnc 300]] Nothing about certificates. [[slnc 700]] On the right, what Caddy made by itself. [[slnc 300]] A certificate for w w w dot shop dot localhost. [[slnc 300]] Signed by Caddy's intermediate. [[slnc 300]] Signed by Caddy's own root, which stays in its data folder. [[slnc 700]] The lifetime, worked out: not after, minus not before, is forty three thousand two hundred seconds. [[slnc 300]] That is twelve hours, and Caddy renews it on its own. [[slnc 700]] Below, what clients see. [[slnc 300]] Caddy's root is needed: the lab's root is refused. [[slnc 300]] And a client that offers only T L S one point two is still served."),
    dict(key='20-act-4-1', kind='console', title='Act Four — Caddy makes its own certificates',
         body='FOUR. Caddy makes its own certificates.\n  THE CADDYFILE (the site block, all of it):\n    www.shop.localhost {\n        bind 127.0.0.1\n        respond "the shop"\n    }\n  THE CHAIN CADDY MADE BY ITSELF:\n    www.shop.localhost  (sent by the server)\n      signed by Caddy Local Authority - ECC Intermediate  (sent)\n      signed by Caddy Local Authority - <year> ECC Root  (root)\n  THE LIFETIME, WORKED OUT (not after - not before):\n    43200 s / 3600 s an hour = 12 hours',
         narration='Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The site block, all of it. [[slnc 400]] The chain Caddy made: the certificate, the intermediate that was sent, and the root. [[slnc 400]] And the lifetime, worked out: twelve hours.'),
    dict(key='21-act-4-2', kind='console', title='Act Four — Caddy makes its own certificates',
         body='FOUR. (continued)\n  THE REQUESTS AND RESPONSES:\n    trusts Caddy\'s root    200 over TLSv1.3\n    trusts the lab\'s root  refused: no trusted root signed it\n    offers only TLS 1.2    200 over TLSv1.2\n    plain http GET /cart   308\n      Location: https://www.shop.localhost/cart\n  BEFORE THE FIX (the headers by default):\n    Server: Caddy\n    Strict-Transport-Security: (none)\n  AFTER THE FIX (one line in the site block):\n    header Strict-Transport-Security\n      "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"\n    Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains',
         narration="The requests. [[slnc 300]] Caddy's root works; the lab's root is refused; one point two is served. [[slnc 400]] Plain http gets three oh eight. [[slnc 400]] Before the fix, the server header says only Caddy, and there is no H S T S. [[slnc 400]] One header line adds it."),
    dict(key='22-act-5-figure', kind='figure', title='No responder, nothing stapled; no reload, the old certificate',
         body='renewal_reload',
         narration="Fifth demo: the bill. [[slnc 400]] At the top, stapling. [[slnc 300]] The server could attach a signed answer that says: this certificate is not revoked. [[slnc 300]] To get one, it needs a responder named in the certificate. [[slnc 300]] Neither nginx's certificate nor Caddy's names one. [[slnc 300]] So nothing is stapled, and nginx says so in its error log. [[slnc 700]] Below, a renewal. [[slnc 300]] A new certificate is written over the old file. [[slnc 300]] On disk, it is the new one. [[slnc 300]] But nginx still sends the old one. [[slnc 600]] After nginx dash s reload, it sends the new one. [[slnc 300]] So a renewal job for nginx must end with a reload. [[slnc 300]] Caddy renews and swaps by itself."),
    dict(key='23-act-5-1', kind='console', title='Act Five — The bill',
         body='FIVE. The bill.\n  THE LINES (stapling: the server attaches a revocation answer):\n    ssl_stapling on;\n    ssl_trusted_certificate <work>/ca/root.crt;\n  THE CERTIFICATES (does one name an OCSP responder?):\n    nginx\'s  no\n    Caddy\'s  no\n  THE RESULT (stapled OCSP answers the client received):\n    nginx  0  <- STAPLING NEEDS A RESPONDER IN THE CERTIFICATE\n    Caddy  0\n  NGINX\'S ERROR LOG:\n    "ssl_stapling" ignored, no OCSP responder URL in the certificate',
         narration='Here is the output. [[slnc 400]] The stapling lines. [[slnc 400]] Does a certificate name an O C S P responder? No, and no. [[slnc 400]] So zero answers are stapled, and the error log says why.'),
    dict(key='24-act-5-2', kind='console', title='Act Five — The bill',
         body='FIVE. (continued)\n  THE RENEWAL (a new certificate written over the old file):\n    on disk          <new serial>\n    served by nginx  <old serial>  <- READ ONLY AT START OR RELOAD\n  AFTER nginx -s reload:\n    served by nginx  <new serial>',
         narration='The renewal. [[slnc 300]] The new serial is on disk, but nginx serves the old one. [[slnc 400]] After the reload, it serves the new one.'),
    dict(key='25-costs', kind='bullets', title='What It Costs',
         body=['nginx: every choice is a line to get right', 'nginx serves a new certificate only after a reload', 'Caddy: its defaults decide for you', 'HSTS is a promise you cannot quickly take back', 'Stapling needs a responder in the certificate'],
         narration='Nothing is free. [[slnc 400]] Here is what it costs. [[slnc 500]] In nginx, every choice is a line you must write, and get right. [[slnc 500]] nginx serves a new certificate only after a reload. [[slnc 500]] Caddy decides for you, so you must know what its defaults are. [[slnc 500]] H S T S is a promise to browsers that you cannot quickly take back. [[slnc 300]] And stapling a revocation answer needs a responder named in the certificate.'),
    dict(key='26-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['Server: nginx/1.x in a response', 'nginx -s reload after a renewal', 'A Caddyfile with only a site name', 'Strict-Transport-Security in a response'],
         narration='You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] A server header with nginx and its exact version is a server that never turned the version off. [[slnc 500]] Nginx dash s reload, after a certificate renewal, is the reload from the last demo. [[slnc 500]] A Caddyfile with nothing but a site name is Caddy deciding the rest. [[slnc 500]] And a strict transport security header is H S T S, telling the browser to use https only.'),
    dict(key='27-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['TLS 1.2 and 1.3 only; 1.3 where you can', 'Redirect http to https', 'HSTS on every page; watch add_header', 'Hide the server version', 'Renew automatically, and reload', 'Test as a client sees it'],
         narration='So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Serve only T L S one point two and one point three, and only one point three where every client can. [[slnc 500]] Redirect plain http to https. [[slnc 300]] Send H S T S on every page, and repeat it in any nginx location that adds its own headers. [[slnc 500]] Hide the server version. [[slnc 300]] Renew certificates automatically, and reload. [[slnc 500]] And test the result the way a client sees it.'),
    dict(key='28-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=['Https: a small client that checks the name it asks for', "ChainCheck: the client's two checks, redone by hand", 'Negotiation: the protocol version both sides agree on', 'Headers: the add_header rule, and what a max-age means', 'Nginx: writes nginx.conf for each case, and runs nginx', 'Caddy: writes a Caddyfile and runs Caddy with its own data folder'],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] Two classes run the real servers, one is a small client, and three small classes redo by hand what the client and the servers decide. [[slnc 300]] Here they are.'),
    dict(key='29-class-1', kind='code', title='Https — server name and name check',
         body='// Https: a small client that checks the name it asks for\np.setServerNames(List.of(new SNIHostName(name)));\np.setEndpointIdentificationAlgorithm("HTTPS");\n...\nif (protocols.length > 0) {\n    p.setProtocols(protocols);\n}\n...\nsocket.startHandshake();',
         narration='The client connects to the loopback address, and sends the name it wants in the handshake. [[slnc 300]] Endpoint identification makes the J D K check that name against the certificate. [[slnc 300]] The protocols line limits what the client offers, to play the old client.'),
    dict(key='30-class-2', kind='code', title='ChainCheck — who signed it, and whom it names',
         body="// ChainCheck: the client's two checks, redone by hand\nboolean named = root.getSubjectX500Principal().equals(issuer);\nif (named && signedBy(top, root)) {\n    trusted = true;\n}\n...\nboolean listed = names(chain.get(0)).contains(name);\n...\ncert.verify(issuer.getPublicKey());",
         narration="A root is trusted when its name is the certificate's issuer, and its public key verifies the signature. [[slnc 300]] The name check looks in the certificate's list of names. [[slnc 300]] A test proves the result matches the J D K's own validator."),
    dict(key='31-class-3', kind='code', title='Negotiation — the highest version in common',
         body='// Negotiation: the protocol version both sides agree on\nfor (String version : VERSIONS) {\n    if (server.contains(version) && client.contains(version)) {\n        return Optional.of(version);\n    }\n}\nreturn Optional.empty();',
         narration='The versions are listed newest first. [[slnc 300]] The first one that both sides speak wins. [[slnc 300]] If there is none, the handshake fails.'),
    dict(key='32-class-4', kind='code', title="Headers — a location's own lines win",
         body='// Headers: the add_header rule, and what a max-age means\nreturn location.isEmpty() ? server : location;\n...\nMatcher m = Pattern.compile("max-age=(\\\\d+)").matcher(hsts);',
         narration="One line holds nginx's rule: the server's add header lines reach a location only when it has none of its own. [[slnc 300]] The max age is read in seconds, and the demo divides it by a day."),
    dict(key='33-class-5', kind='code', title='Nginx — one line per choice, and a reload',
         body='// Nginx: writes nginx.conf for each case, and runs nginx\nssl_certificate %5$s;\nssl_certificate_key %6$s;\nssl_protocols %7$s;\n...\nstatic final String HSTS = "add_header Strict-Transport-Security"\n        + " \\"max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains\\" always;";\n...\nProcs.run(command("-s", "reload"));',
         narration='The configuration is a text block, with one line for each choice the lesson changes. [[slnc 300]] The H S T S line is added to the server block, the account location, or both. [[slnc 300]] A reload runs nginx dash s reload, which is when nginx reads the certificate file again.'),
    dict(key='34-class-6', kind='code', title='Caddy — only the site, and local certificates',
         body='// Caddy: writes a Caddyfile and runs Caddy with its own data folder\nlocal_certs\n...\nwww.shop.localhost {\n    bind 127.0.0.1\n    %s\n    respond "the shop"\n}\n...\npb.environment().put("XDG_DATA_HOME", dir.resolve("data").toString());',
         narration='The Caddyfile names only the site. [[slnc 300]] Local certs tells Caddy to be its own authority. [[slnc 300]] Its data folder holds the keys of that authority, so it must be kept.'),
    dict(key='35-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Add ssl_ciphers and see', 'what a TLS 1.2 client negotiates.'],
         narration="That's T L S servers in practice, with nginx and Caddy. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] Every T L S server is a handful of choices, and each choice is either a line you wrote, or a default someone else made. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Add an S S L ciphers line, and see what a T L S one point two client agrees on. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

VIDEO = {'footer': 'TLS Servers in Practice with nginx and Caddy  ·  Security Basics  ·  Java', 'poster': {'headline': [('TLS WITH', 'text'), ('NGINX AND CADDY', 'accent')], 'taglines': [('Every TLS choice is a line you write, or a default', 'gold')], 'before': {'code': 'Server: nginx/<version>', 'note': 'One line per choice', 'tag': 'BEFORE'}, 'after': {'code': 'ssl_protocols TLSv1.3;', 'note': 'Written, tested, reloaded', 'tag': 'AFTER'}}, 'highlight': {'console_amber': ['THE REQUEST', 'THE CLIENTS', 'THE RESPONSE', 'THE RESULT', 'THE RENEWAL'], 'console_rose': ['BEFORE THE', 'THE TRAP'], 'console_green': ['AFTER'], 'console_cyan': ['THE CERTIFICATE', 'THE NGINX', 'THE CHECKS', 'THE AGREEMENT', 'THE LINE', 'THE max-age', 'THE RULE', 'THE CADDYFILE', 'THE CHAIN', 'THE LIFETIME', "NGINX'S"]}}
